---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="energy-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="energy" from="kcal" to="kj" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert an energy value</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type into either field. Nutrition labels, electricity bills and physics homework use the same control.</li>
      <li>Pick your units from the SI group, the nutrition and heat group, the electrical group, or the mechanical and atomic units.</li>
      <li>The formula panel shows the exact factor with your number in it, so you can copy the working and not only the answer.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Calories, kilocalories and the capital C</h2>
    <p>
      The word "calorie" has two meanings, and they differ by a factor of a thousand. The small calorie is
      the energy needed to warm one gram of water by one degree Celsius: exactly 4.184 joules. The
      kilocalorie is a thousand of those, and nutrition labels mean it when they print "Calories" with a
      capital C.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 500-Calorie meal contains 500 kcal, which is 2,092 kJ, or 2,092,000 J. European labels usually
      give both kJ and kcal; divide the kJ figure by 4.184 to get the number used in the US. As a rough
      mental conversion, divide kJ by 4 and subtract 5 %.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Food</th><th>kcal</th><th>kJ</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Apple</td><td>95</td><td>397</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Slice of bread</td><td>80</td><td>335</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Can of cola</td><td>139</td><td>582</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Chocolate bar (50 g)</td><td>260</td><td>1,088</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Adult daily intake guideline</td><td>2,000–2,500</td><td>8,368–10,460</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Kilowatt-hours and the electricity bill</h2>
    <p>
      A kilowatt-hour is one kilowatt used for one hour: 1,000 W × 3,600 s = 3,600,000 joules. Your meter
      counts it and your tariff prices it. Power is a rate and energy is an amount, so a conversion between
      them needs a time: a 2 kW heater that runs for three hours uses 6 kWh.
    </p>
    <p>
      Useful reference points: 1 kWh is 3,412 BTU, 860 kcal, or 3.6 MJ. A typical UK home uses about
      2,700 kWh of electricity a year; a US home uses about 10,500 kWh. An electric car uses about 15 to
      20 kWh per 100 km.
    </p>

    <h2>BTU, therms and heating</h2>
    <p>
      US heating and air conditioning use British thermal units: the energy to raise one pound of water by
      one degree Fahrenheit, which the international definition fixes at 1,055.05585262 J. Gas bills use
      therms, which are exactly 100,000 BTU, or about 29.3 kWh.
    </p>
    <p>
      To compare gas and electricity prices, convert therms. Gas at 1.20 per therm is 4.1 cents per kWh of
      heat, and a typical electricity price is several times higher. This is the economic case for heat
      pumps, which move three or four units of heat per unit of electricity they use.
    </p>

    <h2>Reference: energy at different scales</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Quantity</th><th>In joules</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>One electronvolt</td><td>1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lifting an apple one metre</td><td>1 J</td></tr>
          <tr><td>One food Calorie (kcal)</td><td>4,184 J</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AA alkaline battery</td><td>about 10,000 J</td></tr>
          <tr><td>One kilowatt-hour</td><td>3,600,000 J</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Litre of petrol burned</td><td>about 34,200,000 J</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Electric car battery (60 kWh)</td><td>216,000,000 J</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The range covers nearly thirty orders of magnitude, so the energy converter changes to scientific
      notation automatically at the extremes. You can also force plain decimal or scientific output with the
      number-format control.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
